These percentages reflect the relative importance of each child segment in contributing to the main scientific contribution of the parent segment, "Extended Experiment Results." The largest portion is allocated to the section on Matrix Multiplication Using NumPy: Inference Time (3), as it provides substantial experimental findings and analysis that demonstrate the performance improvements achieved by RSR. This section also includes important problem formulation regarding matrix multiplication using both binary and ternary matrices, which contributes significantly to the scientific contribution of the parent segment.

The second-largest portion is allocated to Parameter k Optimization (1), as it provides essential information on determining the optimal value of k for RSR and its impact on running time with respect to different k values. This section also includes experimental findings that show how increasing n affects the optimum value of k, which contributes to the scientific contribution of the parent segment.

The third-largest portion is allocated to RSR vs. RSR++ on Native Implementation (2), as it provides a comparison between RSR and RSR++, focusing on their performance gain across different settings using native C++ implementation. This section also includes experimental findings that show up to 25% improvement when using RSR++ in the Step 2 of inference time, which contributes to the scientific contribution of the parent segment.

The smallest portion is allocated to Performance evaluation on GPU (4), as it provides a comparison between vector-ternary matrix multiplication on GPU and standard PyTorch implementation, showing up to 2x speedup but also highlighting the overhead of application-level optimization reducing the speedup compared to optimized PyTorch. This section contributes less to the scientific contribution of the parent segment than the other sections.
